---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { typeGroups, typeHref } from '../data/types';
import '../styles/catalog.css';
import '../styles/prose.css';
---

<Base title="About MailSchema" theme="light">
  <main id="main" class="container">
    <header class="page-hero">
      <span class="eyebrow eyebrow-line">The project</span>
      <h1>About MailSchema</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        MailSchema publishes a working draft for actions described in email and a Registry of shared
        type definitions.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="longform">
      <aside class="longform-aside">
        <span class="eyebrow">MailSchema</span>
        <p>
          Mail Action Protocol
          <br />
          Type registry
        </p>
      </aside>
      <article class="prose">
        <h2>Why email</h2>
        <p>
          Reviews, requests and decisions already arrive in the inbox. A message often contains the
          context an agent needs, but each service describes the next step in its own way.
        </p>
        <p>
          An email address lets a service reach someone across organizations without knowing which
          agent or app they use. The readable message still works in an ordinary mailbox. MAP adds a
          description a connected client can use without treating arrival as permission to act.
        </p>
        <p>
          MAP gives a request a common shape. An agent or inbox can tell what is being asked, then
          check the current proposal with the service before offering a decision.
        </p>
        <h2>The project</h2>
        <p>
          <a href="/specification">Mail Action Protocol</a> defines how an email describes an action
          and what the client and service must check before acting.{' '}
          <a href="/specification/contracts">Type contracts</a> define particular kinds of work. The{' '}
          <a href="/registry">Registry</a> publishes those definitions, versions and maintainers,
          with service support when available.
        </p>
        <p>
          Services retain their own permissions and business operations. People can continue to read
          the email and use the service directly.
        </p>
        <h2>Current work</h2>
        <p>
          The current specification is a working draft. It defines what goes in the email, how a
          connected client checks the current proposal, and what the service must check before
          acting. An experimental binding allows limited responses without signing in, such as
          declining a proposal; it does not authorize consequential approvals. Each type carries its
          own review status. The Registry distinguishes service support claims from observed
          behavior; a listing alone does not establish interoperability.
        </p>
        <p>The initial contracts are:</p>
        <ul>
          {typeGroups.map((group) => (
            <li>
              <strong>{group.status}:</strong>{' '}
              {group.records.map((type, index) => (
                <Fragment>
                  {index > 0 && ', '}
                  <a href={typeHref(type.slug)}>{type.name}</a>
                </Fragment>
              ))}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <h2>Standards and drafts</h2>
        <p>
          MAP uses the proposed Structured Email message structure, DKIM with DMARC alignment for
          message trust, and JSON-LD and JSON Schema for descriptions. It can connect to existing
          service interfaces; HTTP is one specified binding. Structured Email is still a draft, so
          MAP's use of its container needs coordination before RFC publication. Each type proposal
          identifies related work and explains what it adds. The{' '}
          <a href="/specification/sources">standards references</a> explain the role and limits of
          each dependency.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/contribute">Contribute a proposal →</a>
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
